# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: vlax-for-binding-rule-unprobed — VLAX-FOR's loop variable rule is asserted by analogy, not probed
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: divergence
#+DEPENDS: bricscad-access (probe:suite:bricscad:*), autocad COM for the AutoCAD column
#+BRANCH: -

Title: VLAX-FOR's loop variable rule is asserted by analogy, not probed
Status: open (the code is fixed; the EVIDENCE is what is missing)
Found: 2.0.26, while open-coding VLAX-FOR in the transpiler

WHAT WAS WRONG

VLAX-FOR bound its loop variable like this:

    (if (find-dynamic-binding name ...)
        (set-variable name element context)
        (bind-dynamic-variable name element context))

That is ASSIGN-IF-ALREADY-BOUND, and it is exactly the code FOREACH
carried until 2.0.22. In 2.0.22 pjb asked whether that was really how
FOREACH behaves; the probe went to AutoCAD, and AutoCAD answered that
FOREACH BINDS -- which is also what the specification had always said
("bind it to name", "rebinding name for each iteration"). FOREACH was
fixed and the rule was extracted into BIND-FOREACH-VARIABLE so it would
live in one place.

VLAX-FOR was not updated with it. So until 2.0.26 the two loops in this
implementation DISAGREED about their own loop variable:

    (vlax-for x coll ...)   wrote a caller's local named X, and left
                            the last member of the collection in it
    (foreach  x lst ...)    did not

Both now call BIND-FOREACH-VARIABLE.

WHY THIS TICKET STAYS OPEN

The fix is asserted BY ANALOGY, and the analogy is not evidence:

  - two loops in one implementation differing on their binding rule is
    far likelier to be an oversight than an undocumented vendor quirk;
  - the shared code says so: both went through the same
    assign-if-bound lines, and only one of the two was corrected when
    that code was proved wrong;
  - FOREACH's rule was settled by a real engine, and the answer was the
    one the spec had described all along -- so where the spec speaks
    about a loop variable here, it has been shown reliable.

AND THE WEAKNESS OF IT, stated because it is the point of the ticket:
the spec does NOT speak about VLAX-FOR's loop variable. Its entry says
only "Iterates over an ActiveX collection object" -- no `bind', no
`rebinding', nothing about what survives the loop. So the third
argument above supports FOREACH and does not reach VLAX-FOR, and what
is left is an argument from symmetry and from shared history.

That is a good argument. It is not a measurement, and 2.0.22 is the
reason to distrust good arguments about this exact question: the
assign-if-bound behaviour ALSO looked reasonable, was in the code for a
long time, matched nobody, and was only settled by asking a real
engine.

WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT

A probe of the shape probes/sources/probe-foreach-scope.lsp uses, but
over a real COM collection:

    (defun p ( / x)
      (setq x 'before)
      (vlax-for x (vla-get-blocks (vla-get-activedocument (vlax-get-acad-object)))
        nil)
      x)                          ; BEFORE if it binds, a block if it assigns

It has not been written because it needs a live AutoCAD or BricsCAD
with a document open -- the probe suite runs headless, and
*VLAX-COLLECTION-ITEMS-HOOK* cannot be conjured from the harness. It
belongs with the vendor-probe jobs rather than the suite, and should go
in the next time those are touched.

UNTIL THEN, what the tests pin is the ANALOGY, not the vendor: the
compiler equivalence tests install a fake collection hook and assert
that VLAX-FOR and FOREACH agree with each other. If a probe ever
contradicts this, BIND-FOREACH-VARIABLE is the single place to change,
which is the whole reason it exists.

* The probe is written — 2026-09-28

=probes/sources/probe-vlax-for-scope.lsp=, in the manifest after
foreach-scope, so every =probe:suite:*= job runs it. Four cases, the
FOREACH ones transposed: the loop variable a /-local of the same function,
a /-local of the CALLER, a global, and bound nowhere. The collection is the
active document's Layers (layer "0" always exists), and each case returns
=(<variable afterwards> <iterations>)= so that BEFORE cannot be a loop that
never ran; a layer left behind shows as ="layer:<name>"=.

Headless clautolisp baseline (probe-results/clautolisp/linux/20260928T121031Z):

| case                  | clautolisp  | means |
|-----------------------+-------------+-------|
| own /-local           | (BEFORE 1)  | binds |
| caller's /-local      | (BEFORE 1)  | binds |
| global                | (OUTER 1)   | binds |
| bound nowhere         | (nil 1)     | no leak |

What is left is the VENDOR columns:
- =probe:suite:bricscad:macos= / =:windows= -- BricsCAD has the object
  model in its batch runs, so these should answer;
- =probe:suite:autocad:windows= runs accoreconsole, which has no ActiveX
  object model: expect =error= in every case there, which answers nothing
  about the rule. AutoCAD's column needs a full acad.exe run (COM
  automation), currently blocked by alfe-autocad-createobject-fails-on-runner.

Close on the BricsCAD answer if it is BIND on both platforms, with the
AutoCAD column recorded as unavailable; reopen as a divergence if any
engine answers ASSIGN.

** BricsCAD macOS answered: BIND — 2026-09-28

The BricsCAD probe jobs had PROBE_SUITES pinned to the old FOREACH
experiment, so the suite never ran there; MR !347 added it. Then
=probe:suite:bricscad:macos= (job 16779889621, BricsCAD V26,
probe-results/bricscad/macos/20260928T131139Z):

| case                  | BricsCAD macOS | clautolisp  |
|-----------------------+----------------+-------------|
| own /-local           | (BEFORE 1)     | (BEFORE 1)  |
| caller's /-local      | (BEFORE 1)     | (BEFORE 1)  |
| global                | (OUTER 1)      | (OUTER 1)   |
| bound nowhere         | (NIL 1)        | (nil 1)     |

BricsCAD BINDS the VLAX-FOR loop variable, exactly as it does FOREACH's, and
as clautolisp has since 2.0.26. =probe:suite:bricscad:windows= on the same
pipeline was cancelled before it ran (a newer pipeline superseded it); the
Windows column is the last one this ticket waits for.
